CANARY LICENSE

Copyright 2026 the CANARY contributors. Each keeps the copyright in
their own contributions, and Section 1 says whose are licensed.

This file licenses the CANARY repository as decision 0041 maps it
(docs/decisions/0041-license-and-author-order.md). It is not legal
advice.

========================================================================
1. Who licenses what
========================================================================

Four contributors, marek-sm, j0hanj, Dhruv235, and milesshoe, have
posted decision 0041's written agreement on issue #117
(https://github.com/marek-sm/CANARY/issues/117). Their contributions,
through the release tag, are licensed as Section 2 maps them.

Two contributors, nkuhanas and samsonchang2028, have not. Files holding
their surviving lines are not licensed (Section 3).

The sections apply in this order:

  1. Outside material keeps its own terms (Section 4).
  2. A file listed in Section 3 is not licensed, in whole.
  3. CC BY 4.0 covers content (Section 2.2).
  4. Apache-2.0 covers everything else CANARY wrote (Section 2.1).

========================================================================
2. The two licenses
========================================================================

2.1 Apache License 2.0 (full text in Part A) covers code, tests,
schemas, protocol/, configuration, and the corpus and fixture material
CANARY wrote:

  - agent/, analysis/, channels/, defenses/, oracles/, runner/,
    scripts/, tasks/, and tools/
  - demo/*.py and demo/replay_spec.json
  - tests/
  - schemas/
  - protocol/
  - corpus/ and fixtures/. Where a file holds third-party payload
    text, only CANARY's own material in it is licensed here, and the
    payload text keeps its source's terms (Section 4)
  - configuration: .github/, Dockerfile, Makefile, compose.yaml,
    pyproject.toml, uv.lock, .dockerignore, .env.example, .gitignore,
    paper/estimand_manifest.json, and paper/output_pipeline_freeze.json

2.2 Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0, full
text in Part B) covers documentation, docs/evidence/, figures,
captions, video, transcript, generated tables, and sanitized results:

  - docs/, including docs/evidence/
  - every Markdown (.md) file, wherever it sits
  - results/
  - paper/generated/, demo/generated/, and demo/recorded/
  - any figure, caption, video, or transcript, wherever it sits

No license is claimed over model-generated text in any of these files
(Section 4). A manuscript is covered only if its authors agree, and
this file covers none, whatever its format.

For CC BY 4.0 credit, name "the CANARY contributors", link
https://github.com/marek-sm/CANARY and this license, and say what you
changed.

========================================================================
3. Files this file does not license
========================================================================

Under decision 0041 item 5, every file below holds at least one
surviving line by nkuhanas or samsonchang2028, as the second column
says. Neither license in Section 2 covers any part of these files,
including the lines other contributors wrote. They are public, but no
reuse right is granted for them, and every public statement that
CANARY's work can be reused or adopted is limited to the licensed
paths.

The list holds 205 of the 728 files tracked at this commit:

  50ab9a48b2f0057a6a8161623cb076671a33159e

It was found with git blame -M -C -C. A line counts when blame traces
it to one of the 43 commits listed after the file list, which
are every commit in the history authored under either account. One of
them, def33e97b505b19e8e5a66546cccaa5ed538069d, has no linked account
and sits on nkuhanas's pull request #32, so it counts with theirs. To
check the list, run this at the repository root:

  REV=50ab9a48b2f0057a6a8161623cb076671a33159e
  grep -oE '^    [0-9a-f]{40}$' LICENSE | tr -d ' ' > commits.txt
  git ls-tree -r -z --name-only "$REV" | xargs -0 -n1 sh -c '
    git blame -M -C -C --line-porcelain "$0" -- "$1" |
    grep -oE "^[0-9a-f]{40} " | tr -d " " | grep -qxFf commits.txt &&
    printf "%s\n" "$1"' "$REV"

The project lead regenerates this list against the release commit and
merges any correction before the tag. A match is part of the release
checklist's license item.

